  "standard_trained_vs_chance_prediction_finding": {
    "prediction": "standard-trained models should score at CHANCE (0.5) on the hard-negative subset",
    "actual_result": "BIMODAL, not chance -- pearson correlation(positive_accuracy, hard_negative_accuracy) = -0.9979 across all 40 standard-trained seeds (near-perfect NEGATIVE correlation). Zero of 40 seeds land in the 0.4-0.6 near-chance band for hard-negative accuracy (10 seeds <0.1, 18 seeds >0.9, 12 in between but still not clustered near 0.5). Zero seeds achieve BOTH positive_accuracy>0.9 AND hard_negative_accuracy>0.5.",
    "interpretation": "The apparently HIGH hard-negative accuracy seen in many standard-trained seeds (e.g. 9/10 LSTM seeds at 0.80-0.96, all 10 transformer seeds at 0.84-1.00) is NOT residual target-computation capability -- it is the SAME reject-bias artifact documented in F4 for corrected-trained models, now triggered by evaluating standard-trained checkpoints OUT OF DISTRIBUTION on the corrected test set (which contains negative examples statistically unlike anything in their training distribution). The near-perfect negative correlation with positive accuracy is the diagnostic signature: models rejecting the hard-negative half well are, in lockstep, ALSO rejecting genuine positives at a similarly elevated rate -- a generic out-of-distribution reject bias, not selective arithmetic/content verification. The chance-level prediction is WRONG in a specific, well-evidenced way: standard training does not preserve a dormant genuine-content-verification capability that surfaces at 50% on hard negatives; instead it reproduces the exact same shortcut-vs-reject-bias dichotomy seen throughout this whole experiment, this time driven by distribution shift rather than by training-data composition."
  }
